And this is what this talk is about, kind of addressing in a way.

55
00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:53.400
So anybody who's super familiar with the math behind attention

56
00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.200
is probably going to be really bored by this slide,

57
00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.680
but I didn't want to assume that everybody would know exactly how it works,

58
00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.040
because if you don't kind of

59
00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:03.960
have an idea of this diagram, you don't really understand the optimization.

60
00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:07.360
So kind of the intuition

61
00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.360
is obviously the every token that the model is processing.

62
00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:14.000
Attention is basically asking which of the other tokens in this sequence

63
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.080
are relevant to me, and what does that tell me?

64
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.960
So Q is your queries, which is the question that each token is asking.

65
00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.120
K is kind of a label associated with each token, and v

66
00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.400
the value is what that token contributes if it's selected.

67
00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.840
So if you multiply q by the transpose of k,

68
00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.880
you get what we call like a matrix of attention scores.

69
00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:37.600
And this is a sequence length

70
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.440
by sequence length matrix of compatible compatibility.

71
00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:44.680
And then you do an operation called softmax

72
00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:47.840
which we're going to come to to in more detail in a second.

73
00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:52.000
A key in the title of this talk, softmax is kind of important here.

74
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.080
And that gives you basically a probability distribution

75
00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:00.000
Q times the transpose of K is a matrix multiplication, which we really like.

76
00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:03.040
On GPUs we have ten scores that make those really fast.

77
00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:06.520
And then softmax has this exponential.

78
00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.760
And it's interesting because actually on some of our hardware

79
00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.760
we're actually bottleneck by the exponential more than we are

80
00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:15.440
by the matrix multiply

81
00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.320
kind of

82
00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.040
on the right here we have what we call multi-headed attention,

83
00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:24.480
which is this attention process applied to multiple heads in parallel.

84
00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:28.320
Yeah.

85
00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.680
So now a little bit about softmax.

86
00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:33.160
I've just said that softmax is really important.

87
00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:36.680
And the algorithm here like online softmax

88
00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.600
came up with the by guy I've cited on this slide, Maxim Malakhov.

89
00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.160
He's in my team. He's super smart.

90
00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.360
And I think he probably gets some personal satisfaction

91
00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:48.360
every time I mention his paper at one of these talks.

92
00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.640
So the problem with naive

93
00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:55.280
softmax is basically that you need a global max across all of the keys.

94
00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.520
And that means in order to keep your numerical stability

95
00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:00.880
so that you don't overflow.

96
00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:03.960
The problem is you don't know the max until you've already processed

97
00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.520
all the elements, which means you need at least two passes.

98
00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:08.000
And over this big matrix.

99
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:09.760
I think it's really expensive.

100
00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:14.480
So thanks to Maxim, we have this online softmax which allows you to process this

101
00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:15.520
in a single pass.

102
00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.680
And the idea is basically you have to keep a running max and do some checking.

103
00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.680
That means you can do a correction at the end.

104
00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.640
Now this running max really is a super important

105
00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:29.160
because we use it to check whether we can skip the softmax later.

106
00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.920
So we should really pay attention to that.

107
00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.640
So there's kind of three properties to keep in mind.

108
00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:38.680
Single pass.

109
00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:42.440
Which means we don't have to materialize the full attention matrix in memory

110
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:43.440
in any one time.

111
00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.560
This is kind of one of the key optimizations behind flash attention.

112
00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:50.120
It's like reading that matrix over and over again is really expensive.

113
00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:53.280
Numerically stable is super important as well.

114
00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.560
So naive

115
00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:04.960
softmax requires two passes, three operations per tensor element.

116
00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:09.280
Safe softmax subtracts the max to eliminate the overflow,

117
00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:10.320
but that also requires

118
00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.760
that requires three passes and four memory operations per element.

119
00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.800
Online reduces this back to three.

120
00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:21.600
So we talked about attention being expensive.

121
00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:24.800
The really interesting part about attention is that despite

122
00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.080
being super expensive, it's actually also very sparse.

123
00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:31.280
So this kind of diagram on the slide here is showing you

124
00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:34.280
basically the result of queue times k.

125
00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.360
And these kind of squares that are highlighted in

126
00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.560
gray are the ones that are like I don't know really what to say.

127
00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:43.440
It's kind of like an informal term but important.

128
00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.920
So it's kind of weird is that we do all of this work,

129
00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:51.600
and yet a lot of it doesn't really contribute to the result.

130
00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.760
So oftentimes attention I think I've cited on the slide here.

131
00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.880
In some cases it can be up to 96% of your workload.

132
00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.880
So the idea that I'm saying that a lot of this work is actually wasted

133
00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:06.920
is kind of really not ideal, right.

134
00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:16.160
So this is taking a changing of tack a little bit.

135
00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.200
So the kernel I'm trying to say this,

136
00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:22.800
this presentation is basically an exercise in kernel optimization.

