00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.840
And the kernel that I worked on is a kernel from Nvidia

138
00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:29.880
that we called FMA, which is a fuzed multi-headed attention.

139
00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:33.120
It's a bit like flash attention, except I think we support a bit more.

140
00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:36.160
I think a lot of the key optimizations are the same.

141
00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:40.280
And it turns out that actually in order to write a good attention

142
00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.280
kernel, you can't really do that in one kernel.

143
00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:43.680
You need like hundreds of attention.

144
00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:45.880
Kernels are optimized for different scenarios.

145
00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:49.480
So for your context kernel versus your generation

146
00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:52.560
phase kernel require a very different optimizations.

147
00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:56.080
Because when you're doing generation you only generate one token

148
00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:58.840
and then you're attending to the entire previous cave cache.

149
00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:01.840
And that changes the shape of your matrix multiply.

150
00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:05.240
So basically

151
00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:09.040
what we decided to do was we wrote a code generator called TR.

152
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.680
And basically we use that to

153
00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.960
help us maintain all of these kernels because we realized it would be easier

154
00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:17.960
to write one generator and then generate the kernel we need,

155
00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:23.600
versus have me maintain potentially thousands of attention kernels.

156
00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:28.600
I think currently off the top of my head inside tensor RT Elm on GitHub,

157
00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:32.040
I think we have 4000 kernel binaries for attention,

158
00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:35.800
and that often blows up even more when somebody like me comes along

159
00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:38.920
and says, hey, can we get a skips off max variant of all of these, please?

160
00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.280
And then you multiply it by two.

161
00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:46.320
The kernel is warp specialized for anybody who doesn't know what that means.

162
00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.920
A warp is basically a group of 32 threads that operate in parallel on GPU.

163
00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:55.480
And when we say warp specialized, we mean each warp gets a different job to do.

164
00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:00.000
And this is kind of a method to allow us to keep all the different execution

165
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.240
units on the GPU busy.

166
00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:05.920
So memory may your softmax unit, not softmax unit

167
00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:09.400
and multifunction unit, which you used to calculate softmax.

168
00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.360
So for example, in attention, in flash attention, often what happens

169
00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:17.120
is we use one warp to load tiles of the matrices, another warp.

170
00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:18.640
What we do in matrix multiplies.

171
00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:20.920
Somebody else will be doing softmax, and then somebody else

172
00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:22.160
is writing out the output.

173
00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:26.760
And kind of synchronizing all of these very different stages of the pipeline

174
00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:31.360
is super hard to do by hand, which also kind of lends itself to a code generation

175
00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:36.720
kind of method just because it's so much to holding your head

176
00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.880
as a programmer, I don't necessarily think it's feasible to do by yourself.

177
00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:44.000
So there are more

178
00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:47.240
tasks in the kernel than listed on this slide.

179
00:10:47.240 --> 00:10:49.160
But basically when I say a task,

180
00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:52.160
I mean, this is something we're getting a specialized warp to do.

181
00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:55.480
And the key ones that we care about are kind of listed in this table.

182
00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:57.560
So like the load task does.

183
00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:58.800
Well, it's kind of in the name really.

184
00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:00.960
It loads data from global memory.

185
00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.680
And this is basically for reading QQ and V we have the main task

186
00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:09.920
which issues instructions to the tensor cores softmax task.

187
00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:13.640
And that reads the result of what we call BM one which means

188
00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:17.880
batch to matrix multiply and BM one refers to q times k.

189
00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.280
So if I say bm one, that's what I mean.

190
00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:24.720
If I say two, it's the p times v matrix multiply.

191
00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:28.520
And we finally have the correction task which is relevant to

192
00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.760
Maxim's online softmax.

193
00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:34.040
So it's kind of.

194
00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:39.720
Get an idea of why this works or what this optimization is going to be.

195
00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:43.560
And I promise this is the only maths we're going to do together

196
00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.080
in this presentation.

197
00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:49.320
I said before that a lot of the attention values that we are

198
00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:53.480
calculating are end up being zero after softmax or close to zero.

199
00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:57.240
So that means then when you do your p times v bm two,

200
00:11:57.280 --> 00:11:59.440
that's something interesting happens.

201
00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:03.320
Say you have a row of this matrix that is all zeros.

202
00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:06.000
What does that mean for your result.

203
00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.760
Well it turns out that that also means

204
00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:12.480
that the result of that matrix multiply for that row is also zero.

205
00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:15.440
So what if I told you we could just, you know, not bother doing it?

206
00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:16.440
Because what's the point.

207
00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:18.480
It's not changing the answer anyway.

208
00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:23.400
So I think anybody who's familiar with flash

209
00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:26.400
attention would have seen a diagram like this before.

210
00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:30.880
Basically, to avoid realizing

211
00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:34.080
the full attention matrix in memory, we break it up into tiles.

212
00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:34.840
That's kind of shown

213
00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:38.520
in this diagram here, where you do one q times k for a given tile,

214
00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:43.280
and then you softmax that tile, multiply it by v and accumulate it into the output.

215
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:46.240
So that's kind of the loop.

216
00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:49.240
It makes it very memory efficient.

217
00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:54.200
What it doesn't do, however, is work out whether that attention calculation

218
00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:57.280
is actually worth doing, which is kind of what I keep coming back to.

219
00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:01.640
So many of these tiles are producing softmax values that are essentially zero

220
00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:03.160
or close to zero.

221
00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:05.040
And something, something that we'll come back to you

222
00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:08.120
later is the idea that you can tune how aggressive this skip is,

223
00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:11.520
but that obviously has trade offs in terms of accuracy and performance.

224
00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:14.880
So this isn't a corner case either.

225
00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:18.120
Like I said before, attention is sparse.

226
00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:22.360
If we go back to that vertical slash diagram from before, most of those are

227
00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:25.680
squares were white not gray.

228
00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:30.480
So essentially we have a really complicated kernel that processes

229
00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:35.440
loads of tiles, but a workload where most of those tiles don't actually matter.

230
00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:38.680
And we call this kind of skip softmax.

231
00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:42.840
It turns out, actually, that we're skipping a lot more than the softmax.

232
00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:44.680
And oftentimes

233
00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:47.880
softmax is not actually the most expensive operation that we're skipping.

234
00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.880
But, well, we've named it now. So

235
00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:55.520
so the key insight here is how can we skip the tiles

236
00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:59.480
cheaply using information that we've already calculated.

237
00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:03.600
We have this running max that we're keeping for softmax for online softmax.

238
00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:08.080
And that's that's a result of q times k which we've already done.

239
00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:12.320
So kind of calculating this value is not additional work.

240
00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:17.160
So when a new tile arrives we do this batch matrix multiply.

241
00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:21.560
And you take a row wise maximum of each tile that's a local max.

242
00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:23.280
And you compare that to the running max.

243
00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:26.280
Now this is cheap because we were doing this reduction anyway.

244
00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:31.320
Anyway we see in this kind of equation here, the key is

245
00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:35.120
if your local max is much less than your running max,

246
00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:38.960
and by much less I mean the difference would be a large negative number.

247
00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:42.560
Then the result of the exponential is basically zero.

248
00:14:43.720 --> 00:14:44.480
Now that means

249
00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:48.040
if all of the softmax values for that tile would be near zero,

250
00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:51.120
that entire tile contributes nothing to the output.

251
00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:55.120
So we can just set them to zero and not do a bunch of extra work.

252
00:14:55.120 --> 00:14:57.360
So if you kind of look at this diagram here,

253
00:14:57.360 --> 00:15:01.200
you're asking yourself this question have we triggered this threshold or not?

254
00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:05.480
If we haven't triggered it then we're very sad and we have to do all this work.

255
00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:09.480
And if we do trigger it, we can skip the exponential, the normalization

256
00:15:09.480 --> 00:15:13.360
loading of the v tile, a matrix multiply, and the correction step.

257
00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:16.040
And the kind of really nice thing about this

258
00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:18.440
is that you don't need to do any additional training on your models.

259
00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:19.800
It's just an inference time thing.

260
00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:22.360
You can just deploy it and use it

261
00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:25.640
so you save in various ways really.

262
00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:28.640
And that's kind of what this slide is trying to say.

263
00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:31.160
You may hear like a kernel developer

264
00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:34.160
talk about a kernel being compute bound versus memory bound.

265
00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:38.240
When we say that if you're compute bound, it means you spend most much of your time

266
00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:40.160
doing math. And that's kind of your bottleneck.

267
00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:41.320
Whereas if you're memory bound,

268
00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:44.440
you're kind of bottlenecked by, well, the memory subsystem.

269
00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:47.880
Now, if we kind of walk through this, basically

270
00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:51.040
what I've got is a diagram here that shows you all the different parts.

271
00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:55.360
I've just said we're skipping and what exactly you save from skipping them.

272
00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:59.080
So when you skip the softmax and the normalization, that's a compute saving.

273
00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:02.280
You also have to keep statistics to do your correction.

274
00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:04.160
That's also a compute saving.

275
00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:08.680
The really interesting one is the V tile because that's the only memory

276
00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:09.800
saving here.

277
00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:13.840
And in fact for some some versions of this kernel, we don't actually skip this

278
00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:18.080
because if it's a compute bound kernel you're not bottlenecked by that load.

279
00:16:18.200 --> 00:16:21.280
So delaying the war but then has to decide whether to skip

280
00:16:21.280 --> 00:16:24.600
or not actually hurts you more because it doesn't buy you anything.

281
00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:27.240
In a non memory bound situation,

282
00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:31.440
P times V saves you tensor cores, which obviously we care about.

283
00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:34.520
And then the correction we've kind of already talked about.

284
00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:39.800
Interestingly skipping the correction is an optimization that already existed.

285
00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:43.760
We did it prior and I think flash attention does it as well.

286
00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:45.680
But it turns out if you're skipping softmax

287
00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:48.320
you can also combine it with this other optimization.

288
00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:52.360
So kind of the skip condition like how does it work?

289
00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:56.400
I said before, a warp is basically a group of 32 threads.

290
00:16:56.680 --> 00:17:00.000
So each thread needs to decide whether to individually skip,

291
00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.480
because the way we kind of write this kernel or the version

292
00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:06.880
of the kernel that I care about, each thread basically has a row of the tile.

293
00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:10.920
When you're doing this softmax calculation, and you do this exponential

294
00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:14.960
that is effectively measuring how much smaller is this tile

295
00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:17.240
maximum versus the running global max.

296
00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:20.040
And this threshold is is user tunable.

297
00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:23.600
It turns out actually that picking a good value for the threshold is really hard.

298
00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:24.800
When I was

299
00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:27.760
when I was developing the kernel, I didn't really fully appreciate that.

300
00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:27.960
Right?

301
00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:31.240
I would just set a value for my unit test to check it against a reference

302
00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:32.600
and then go, great.

303
00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:36.320
Then we shipped it to production and someone came to me and said, this

304
00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:39.680
is wonderful, but you haven't accounted for the context length at all.

305
00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:43.400
So it turns out that when when I'm trying to set a threshold for this kernel,

306
00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:47.400
I have to change it all the time because it's not normalized.

307
00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:50.120
So then I had to go away and go, okay, well, fine.

308
00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:52.280
Let's normalize by the sequence length of kV,

309
00:17:52.280 --> 00:17:54.200
which is where the divide in that slide comes from.

310
00:17:54.200 --> 00:17:55.440
Was basically because somebody sent me

311
00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:58.120
an angry message on slack and said, this is really hard to use.

312
00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:01.360
So after

313
00:18:01.360 --> 00:18:05.200
that, basically what you need is all of the threads

314
00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:08.840
or all of the warps that are processing a tile need to agree.

315
00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:12.640
So to do that within a warp we have this handy intrinsic called all sync,

316
00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:16.000
which is really fast, but it turns out to do softmax effectively.

317
00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:18.800
You need four warps and multiple warps.

318
00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:21.240
Can't talk to each other, so you have to do some atomics

319
00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:25.000
and shared memory, which is a bit slower, but that's just the way it is.

