00:20:02.320 --> 00:20:04.080
We have what we call sparsity.

353
00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:08.280
And sparsity is effectively what percentage of the attention tiles

354
00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:09.320
have we skipped.

355
00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:13.520
So 100% is we did no work and 0% is we didn't skip at all.

356
00:20:14.840 --> 00:20:16.280
You can never skip the first tile.

357
00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:17.960
Otherwise the threshold check doesn't work.

358
00:20:17.960 --> 00:20:21.680
And I suspect that if you skip all of the tiles, everything

359
00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:23.440
probably collapses into garbage anyway.

360
00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:26.800
So I think the extremes are either end of this graph are not particularly useful.

361
00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:30.400
What we care about probably is this this area in the middle.

362
00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:34.600
Speedups are lower than what we got for BFF 16.

363
00:20:34.600 --> 00:20:37.080
And that's kind of a consistent theme throughout this talk.

364
00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:40.080
And we're still kind of investigating that

365
00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:45.280
generation is a very different kernel to to a pre fill.

366
00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:50.840
It turns out basically because you're attending to the entire kV cache,

367
00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:54.040
but you're only receiving or processing one new token.

368
00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:56.160
Your Q matrix is really skinny.

369
00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:57.600
And the work then becomes

370
00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:01.360
dominated by loading k and v instead of your matrix multiply.

371
00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:02.920
This kind of

372
00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:06.920
brings us back to the cascade like savings diagram I had before.

